22
00:01:50.611 --> 00:01:52.616
A story: the Diwali backlog.

23
00:01:52.896 --> 00:01:59.744
The order platform published every order event to one Kafka topic, orders, with 6 partitions and no key.

24
00:02:00.024 --> 00:02:01.645
Six services consumed it.

25
00:02:01.925 --> 00:02:04.955
On Diwali eve, four things went wrong within an hour:

26
00:02:05.235 --> 00:02:06.707
Wrong order, wrong state.

27
00:02:06.987 --> 00:02:11.083
With no key, the producer spread one order's events across partitions.

28
00:02:11.363 --> 00:02:19.576
Picking processed ORDERCANCELLED before ORDERPLACED for 1,900 orders, and packed groceries for orders that had been cancelled.

29
00:02:20.926 --> 00:02:23.507
One bad message stopped a whole partition.

30
00:02:23.787 --> 00:02:28.502
An event with a malformed GST field made the invoicing consumer throw.

31
00:02:28.782 --> 00:02:36.675
The default error handler retried it forever, partition 3 stopped, and invoices for a sixth of all orders were 4 hours behind.

32
00:02:36.955 --> 00:02:38.299
Rebalancing storms.

33
00:02:38.579 --> 00:02:42.569
The team scaled the notification consumers from 6 to 12 pods.

34
00:02:42.849 --> 00:02:45.579
Only 6 could ever be busy (6 partitions).

35
00:02:45.859 --> 00:02:58.211
Each pod joining triggered a stop-the-world rebalance under the eager assignor, and slow processing exceeded max.poll.interval.ms, which kicked consumers out and triggered more rebalances.

36
00:02:58.491 --> 00:03:01.350
SMS confirmations were 40 minutes late.

37
00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:04.172
Acknowledged, then lost.

38
00:03:04.452 --> 00:03:06.628
The producer used acks=1.

39
00:03:06.908 --> 00:03:17.169
A broker died during the spike, and the follower elected in its place was a few hundred milliseconds behind: ~3,000 acknowledged events were gone, silently.

40
00:03:17.449 --> 00:03:19.049
Nothing was wrong with Kafka.

41
00:03:19.329 --> 00:03:22.508
Every failure was a design choice left at its default:

42
00:03:22.788 --> 00:03:28.996
Key by aggregate id (the order id), so all events of one order stay in one partition, in order.

43
00:03:29.276 --> 00:03:37.063
Poison messages go to a retry topic and then a dead-letter topic, with bounded retries, instead of blocking a partition.

44
00:03:38.413 --> 00:03:47.095
Size partitions for peak consumer parallelism, use the cooperative-sticky assignor, and keep per-record processing well under the poll interval.

45
00:03:47.375 --> 00:03:58.170
Producers use acks=all + idempotence, and topics use replication.factor=3, min.insync.replicas=2, no unclean election.

46
00:03:58.450 --> 00:04:01.074
Consumers are idempotent (Ch 13).

47
00:04:02.424 --> 00:04:03.107
Quote:

48
00:04:03.387 --> 00:04:05.051
The thesis of this chapter.

49
00:04:05.331 --> 00:04:17.981
A message broker gives you decoupling in time (producers don't wait for consumers) and fan-out, and in return makes you design for ordering, duplicates, poison messages, lag and retention explicitly.

50
00:04:18.261 --> 00:04:23.787
Pick the model (queue or log), then pick keys, partitions, acks and commit points on purpose.

51
00:04:24.067 --> 00:04:25.965
Each default trades away one of them.

