120
00:07:13.139 --> 00:07:15.037
Core Concepts, Beginner to Deep.

121
00:07:16.287 --> 00:07:19.722
Intuition: the kitchen order rail versus the ledger book.

122
00:07:20.002 --> 00:07:23.373
A busy restaurant kitchen has two ways to pass orders on.

123
00:07:23.653 --> 00:07:30.053
The order rail (a queue): the waiter clips a ticket on the rail, and whichever cook is free takes it off and cooks it.

124
00:07:30.333 --> 00:07:32.082
More cooks means faster service.

125
00:07:32.362 --> 00:07:34.367
Once a ticket is taken, it's gone.

126
00:07:34.647 --> 00:07:38.615
The pastry chef who wants to know what was ordered today can't look at the rail.

127
00:07:39.965 --> 00:07:44.019
The order book (a log): every order is written on the next line of a book.

128
00:07:44.299 --> 00:07:50.805
The kitchen reads from its bookmark, the bar reads from its own bookmark, and the accountant reads the whole book at night.

129
00:07:51.085 --> 00:07:55.651
Nobody tears pages out; the book is archived after a month (retention).

130
00:07:55.931 --> 00:07:59.856
A new sous-chef can read from page 1 to learn the day (replay).

131
00:08:00.136 --> 00:08:04.360
Duplicates: the waiter, unsure the kitchen heard, shouts the order twice.

132
00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:10.251
The kitchen must recognize table 7, order 41 as already cooking (idempotency).

133
00:08:11.601 --> 00:08:18.619
Ordering: a table's no onions note must reach the same cook who is cooking that table's dish, after the dish order.

134
00:08:18.899 --> 00:08:22.633
So a table's notes go to that table's cook (partitioning by key).

135
00:08:23.983 --> 00:08:26.692
Why messaging: what a broker buys and costs.

136
00:08:26.972 --> 00:08:28.337
