162
00:09:21.591 --> 00:09:24.321
Retries, DLQs, poison-message handling.

163
00:09:25.671 --> 00:09:31.175
Use synchronous calls when the caller needs the answer now: price, stock check, payment result.

164
00:09:31.455 --> 00:09:37.770
Use messaging for facts other services react to, and for work that can happen later (Ch 17).

165
00:09:39.120 --> 00:09:40.251
Queue versus log.

166
00:09:40.531 --> 00:09:41.896
Here is a quick table:

167
00:09:42.176 --> 00:09:50.645
Queue (RabbitMQ, SQS, ActiveMQ, JetStream work-queue), and Log (Kafka, Pulsar, Kinesis, JetStream limits, Redis Streams).

168
00:09:50.925 --> 00:09:52.141
After consumption.

169
00:09:52.421 --> 00:09:56.347
Queue (RabbitMQ, SQS, ActiveMQ, JetStream work-queue):

170
00:09:56.627 --> 00:09:58.120
Message removed on ack.

171
00:09:58.400 --> 00:10:02.283
Log (Kafka, Pulsar, Kinesis, JetStream limits, Redis Streams):

172
00:10:02.563 --> 00:10:05.421
Record stays until retention or compaction.

173
00:10:05.701 --> 00:10:06.640
Consumers.

174
00:10:06.920 --> 00:10:10.845
Queue (RabbitMQ, SQS, ActiveMQ, JetStream work-queue):

175
00:10:11.125 --> 00:10:13.216
Competing consumers share messages.

176
00:10:13.496 --> 00:10:17.379
Log (Kafka, Pulsar, Kinesis, JetStream limits, Redis Streams):

177
00:10:17.659 --> 00:10:19.963
Each consumer group has its own offset.

178
00:10:20.243 --> 00:10:22.205
Within a group, partitions are shared.

179
00:10:22.485 --> 00:10:23.531
Parallelism.

180
00:10:23.811 --> 00:10:27.736
Queue (RabbitMQ, SQS, ActiveMQ, JetStream work-queue):

181
00:10:28.016 --> 00:10:29.445
Add consumers freely.

182
00:10:29.725 --> 00:10:33.608
Log (Kafka, Pulsar, Kinesis, JetStream limits, Redis Streams):

183
00:10:33.888 --> 00:10:35.936
Bounded by partition count per group.

184
00:10:36.216 --> 00:10:37.005
Ordering.

185
00:10:37.285 --> 00:10:41.211
Queue (RabbitMQ, SQS, ActiveMQ, JetStream work-queue):

186
00:10:41.491 --> 00:10:44.904
Usually FIFO per queue, lost with competing consumers.

187
00:10:45.184 --> 00:10:49.067
Log (Kafka, Pulsar, Kinesis, JetStream limits, Redis Streams):

188
00:10:49.347 --> 00:10:50.883
Guaranteed per partition.

189
00:10:51.163 --> 00:10:52.784
Per-message ack or retry.

190
00:10:53.064 --> 00:10:56.989
Queue (RabbitMQ, SQS, ActiveMQ, JetStream work-queue):

191
00:10:57.269 --> 00:10:59.979
Native (nack, requeue, TTL, DLX).

192
00:11:00.259 --> 00:11:04.141
Log (Kafka, Pulsar, Kinesis, JetStream limits, Redis Streams):

193
00:11:04.421 --> 00:11:08.795
Offset-based: a stuck record blocks its partition unless you move it aside.

194
00:11:10.145 --> 00:11:10.998
Replay.

195
00:11:11.278 --> 00:11:15.203
Queue (RabbitMQ, SQS, ActiveMQ, JetStream work-queue):

196
00:11:15.483 --> 00:11:16.977
No (once acked it's gone).

197
00:11:17.257 --> 00:11:21.139
Log (Kafka, Pulsar, Kinesis, JetStream limits, Redis Streams):

198
00:11:21.419 --> 00:11:23.702
Yes: seek to an offset or timestamp.

199
00:11:23.982 --> 00:11:25.091
Typical use.

200
00:11:25.371 --> 00:11:29.297
Queue (RabbitMQ, SQS, ActiveMQ, JetStream work-queue):

201
00:11:29.577 --> 00:11:33.779
Jobs and commands: send SMS, generate invoice PDF.

202
00:11:34.059 --> 00:11:37.942
Log (Kafka, Pulsar, Kinesis, JetStream limits, Redis Streams):

203
00:11:38.222 --> 00:11:42.105
Events and streams: order lifecycle, CDC, telemetry.

204
00:11:43.455 --> 00:11:51.668
L1 shows both: in the queue, a late subscriber gets null, and in the log, analytics replays from offset 0 a month later.

205
00:11:51.948 --> 00:11:52.993
The lines blur:

206
00:11:53.273 --> 00:11:58.820
Kafka 4.x adds share groups (queue semantics on a topic, KIP-932).

207
00:11:59.100 --> 00:12:02.257
JetStream offers both work-queue and limits retention.

208
00:12:02.537 --> 00:12:04.159
RabbitMQ has streams.

209
00:12:04.439 --> 00:12:10.839
The questions stay the same: do messages disappear on ack, and is parallelism bounded by partitions?

210
00:12:12.189 --> 00:12:13.575
Kafka's data model.

211
00:12:13.855 --> 00:12:16.693
Topic: a named stream, split into partitions.

212
00:12:16.973 --> 00:12:21.794
Each partition is an ordered, append-only log of records with increasing offsets.

213
00:12:22.074 --> 00:12:24.421
Record: key, value, headers, timestamp.

214
00:12:24.701 --> 00:12:32.530
The key decides the partition: murmur2(key) mod partitions for keyed records (L2 uses the real client code).

215
00:12:32.810 --> 00:12:37.951
Records without keys go to a sticky partition, per batch, not round-robin per record.

216
00:12:38.231 --> 00:12:40.087
Ordering is per partition only.

217
00:12:40.367 --> 00:12:45.039
All events for ord-1001 go to partition 6 of 12, in order.

218
00:12:45.319 --> 00:12:49.522
Events for different orders on different partitions have no relative order.

219
00:12:50.872 --> 00:12:52.664
Adding partitions remaps keys:

220
00:12:52.944 --> 00:12:58.853
L2 measured 50.1 % of order ids moving from 12 to 24 partitions.

221
00:12:59.133 --> 00:13:03.421
During the change, an order's new events can be consumed before its old ones.

222
00:13:03.701 --> 00:13:09.781
Pick partition counts with headroom up front (§2.12), or migrate to a new topic.

223
00:13:10.061 --> 00:13:19.491
Keys can skew: keying by storeid put the mega store's 5 % of traffic on one partition (1.87x the average of 24).

224
00:13:19.771 --> 00:13:25.573
That's the Ch 11 hot-key problem again, and one consumer in the group becomes the bottleneck.

225
00:13:26.923 --> 00:13:44.395
Segments and retention: each partition is stored as segment files. retention.ms or retention.bytes delete old segments (time or size), and cleanup.policy=compact keeps the latest record per key (plus tombstones with null values for deletes).

226
00:13:44.675 --> 00:13:52.398
Compacted topics are tables in stream form, used for the latest stock per SKU, config and CDC snapshots.

227
00:13:52.678 --> 00:14:01.787
The lab shows the same idea with JetStream's max-msgs-per-subject=1: three statuses for ord-1 became one (DELIVERED).

228
00:14:03.137 --> 00:14:06.529
Replication, ISR, acks and min.insync.replicas.

229
00:14:06.809 --> 00:14:12.633
Each partition has replicas on different brokers: one leader takes writes and followers fetch from it.

230
00:14:12.913 --> 00:14:20.167
ISR (in-sync replicas): the replicas that have caught up to the leader within replica.lag.time.max.ms.

231
00:14:20.447 --> 00:14:24.500
A slow or cut-off follower is removed and rejoins when caught up.

232
00:14:24.780 --> 00:14:29.729
High watermark (HW): the highest offset that all ISR members have.

233
00:14:30.009 --> 00:14:35.364
Consumers only see records below the HW, the committed records. acks (producer):

234
00:14:35.644 --> 00:14:37.308
0 means fire-and-forget.

235
00:14:37.588 --> 00:14:43.455
1 means acked after the leader writes it. all means acked after all current ISR members have it.

236
00:14:44.805 --> 00:14:54.511
min.insync.replicas (topic or broker): with acks=all, the leader rejects writes (NotEnoughReplicas) when the ISR is smaller than this.

237
00:14:54.791 --> 00:15:00.167
Without it, acks=all can silently degrade to leader only when followers fall out.

238
00:15:00.447 --> 00:15:06.570
Unclean leader election: if no ISR member is alive, allow an out-of-sync replica to lead.

239
00:15:06.850 --> 00:15:09.922
You get availability back and lose whatever it didn't have.

240
00:15:10.202 --> 00:15:16.154
L3 ran these configs under crashes and network cuts (RF=3, 2 M records):

241
00:15:17.504 --> 00:15:18.869
Here is a quick table:

242
00:15:19.149 --> 00:15:23.693
Config, and Acked-but-lost, and Rejected, and Notes. acks=0.

243
00:15:23.973 --> 00:15:25.083
Acked-but-lost:

244
00:15:25.363 --> 00:15:27.880
31,592.

245
