Notes:

265
00:16:08.277 --> 00:16:18.197
Online sooner (2,065 versus 30,432 offline ticks), at a cost. acks=all, min.insync=2.

266
00:16:18.477 --> 00:16:19.587
Acked-but-lost:

267
00:16:19.867 --> 00:16:20.613
0.

268
00:16:20.893 --> 00:16:21.811
Rejected:

269
00:16:22.091 --> 00:16:25.184
235,261.

270
00:16:25.464 --> 00:16:26.275
Notes:

271
00:16:26.555 --> 00:16:31.312
Writes refused while ISR 2: the producer knows and retries or fails.

272
00:16:32.662 --> 00:16:37.142
The scripted case shows why min.insync.replicas=2 matters.

273
00:16:37.422 --> 00:16:43.011
Both followers fall out of the ISR, 1,000 writes arrive, then the leader's disk dies.

274
00:16:43.291 --> 00:16:49.969
With min.insync=1, all 1,000 acknowledged writes are gone (their only copy was on that disk).

275
00:16:50.249 --> 00:16:56.009
With min.insync=2, those writes were refused, so 0 acknowledged writes were lost.

276
00:16:57.359 --> 00:17:08.772
The standard durable setup is replication.factor=3, min.insync.replicas=2, acks=all, unclean.leader.election.enable=false.

277
00:17:09.052 --> 00:17:15.985
It survives one broker loss with no data loss, and it refuses writes rather than lying when two are unavailable.

278
00:17:16.265 --> 00:17:24.372
(Kafka 4.x adds eligible leader replicas, KIP-966, to reduce the last replica standing risk further.).

279
00:17:25.722 --> 00:17:27.237
Delivery semantics, then.

280
00:17:27.517 --> 00:17:28.882
Here is a quick table:

281
00:17:29.162 --> 00:17:35.541
Semantics, and How it happens, and L4 measurement (1 M records, crashes in 2 % of batches).

282
00:17:35.821 --> 00:17:36.845
At most once.

283
00:17:37.125 --> 00:17:38.191
How it happens:

284
00:17:38.471 --> 00:17:44.061
Commit the offset before processing (or auto-commit on poll); a crash skips the rest of the batch.

285
00:17:44.341 --> 00:17:48.565
L4 measurement (1 M records, crashes in 2 % of batches):

286
00:17:48.845 --> 00:17:51.853
8,413 never processed.

287
00:17:52.133 --> 00:17:53.178
At least once.

288
00:17:53.458 --> 00:17:54.525
How it happens:

289
00:17:54.805 --> 00:17:58.815
Commit after processing; a crash reprocesses since the last commit.

290
00:17:59.095 --> 00:18:03.319
L4 measurement (1 M records, crashes in 2 % of batches):

291
00:18:03.599 --> 00:18:06.842
9,362 processed twice.

292
00:18:07.122 --> 00:18:08.295
Effectively once.

293
00:18:08.575 --> 00:18:09.642
How it happens:

294
00:18:09.922 --> 00:18:16.941
At-least-once delivery + idempotent effect, or offset stored in the same DB transaction as the effect.

295
00:18:17.221 --> 00:18:21.445
L4 measurement (1 M records, crashes in 2 % of batches):

296
00:18:21.725 --> 00:18:22.919
0 and 0.

297
00:18:24.269 --> 00:18:26.488
Kafka's exactly-once has two parts:

298
00:18:26.768 --> 00:18:36.944
The idempotent producer (enable.idempotence=true, default since 3.0): the broker dedups retries by producer id and sequence number.

299
00:18:37.224 --> 00:18:38.077
L3:

300
00:18:38.357 --> 00:18:40.939
30 duplicates without it, 0 with it.

301
00:18:41.219 --> 00:18:47.875
Transactions (transactional.id): write to several partitions and commit consumer offsets atomically.

302
00:18:48.155 --> 00:18:51.973
Consumers must use isolation.level=readcommitted.

303
00:18:53.323 --> 00:18:59.382
Together that's exactly-once for Kafka-to-Kafka read-process-write (Kafka Streams' exactlyoncev2).

304
00:18:59.662 --> 00:19:11.715
The moment the effect is a database row, an email or an API call, you're back to Ch 13: an inbox or an idempotent effect, or storing offsets transactionally with the effect.

305
00:19:13.065 --> 00:19:14.367
Ordering guarantees.

306
00:19:14.647 --> 00:19:18.039
Per-partition order holds for records from one producer.

307
00:19:18.319 --> 00:19:23.396
With idempotence on, it holds even with retries and up to 5 in-flight requests.

308
00:19:23.676 --> 00:19:29.201
Choose the key = the entity whose events must stay in order, usually the aggregate id.

309
00:19:29.481 --> 00:19:30.889
Things that break order:

310
00:19:31.169 --> 00:19:33.217
Changing the partition count (L2:

311
00:19:33.497 --> 00:19:35.204
50 % of keys move).

312
00:19:35.484 --> 00:19:41.521
Producers without idempotence and with max.in.flight 1 (a retry lands after a later batch).

313
00:19:42.871 --> 00:19:48.439
Retry topics (L4: the failed event was applied after later ones for the same order).

314
00:19:48.719 --> 00:19:51.386
Several outbox relays (Ch 13:

315
00:19:51.666 --> 00:19:52.861
6 inversions).

316
00:19:53.141 --> 00:19:56.255
Consumers processing one partition with a thread pool.

317
00:19:56.535 --> 00:20:04.599
Defend in consumers anyway: carry a per-aggregate version, and apply only newer ones (update... where version:v, Ch 12).

318
00:20:04.879 --> 00:20:07.503
Then reordering costs a skip, not corruption.

319
00:20:08.853 --> 00:20:10.709
Consumer groups and rebalancing.

320
00:20:10.989 --> 00:20:17.368
A consumer group divides partitions among its members: each partition is read by one member of the group.

321
00:20:17.648 --> 00:20:20.229
More members than partitions leaves some idle.

322
00:20:20.509 --> 00:20:24.925
That's why §1.1's 12 pods on 6 partitions didn't help.

323
00:20:25.205 --> 00:20:34.229
Rebalancing happens when members join or leave, or when one misses max.poll.interval.ms because processing took too long between polls.

324
00:20:34.509 --> 00:20:39.885
L2 ran the real assignors for 12 partitions when a 4th consumer joins 3:

325
00:20:41.235 --> 00:20:42.601
Here is a quick table:

326
00:20:42.881 --> 00:20:47.019
Assignor, and Partitions that changed owner, and Protocol. range.

327
00:20:47.299 --> 00:20:49.049
Partitions that changed owner:

328
00:20:49.329 --> 00:20:50.075
6.

329
00:20:50.355 --> 00:20:51.251
Protocol:

330
00:20:51.531 --> 00:20:57.142
Eager: every member revokes everything, then gets a new assignment (stop-the-world). roundrobin.

331
00:20:57.422 --> 00:20:59.171
Partitions that changed owner:

332
00:20:59.451 --> 00:21:00.198
9.

333
00:21:00.478 --> 00:21:01.374
Protocol:

334
00:21:01.654 --> 00:21:03.446
Eager. cooperative-sticky.

335
00:21:03.726 --> 00:21:05.475
Partitions that changed owner:

336
00:21:05.755 --> 00:21:07.355
3 (9, 10, 11).

337
00:21:07.635 --> 00:21:08.531
Protocol:

338
00:21:08.811 --> 00:21:11.115
Incremental: members keep what they own.

339
00:21:11.395 --> 00:21:16.579
In round 1 only 3 partitions are revoked, and round 2 hands them to c4.

340
00:21:17.929 --> 00:21:18.911
Practices:

341
00:21:19.191 --> 00:21:27.468
Use cooperative-sticky, or the new consumer protocol (KIP-848, server-side assignment, Kafka 4.x).

342
00:21:27.748 --> 00:21:32.868
Set static membership (group.instance.id) so a rolling restart doesn't rebalance.

343
00:21:33.148 --> 00:21:40.572
Keep processing per poll well below max.poll.interval.ms (smaller max.poll.records, async hand-off with care).

344
00:21:40.852 --> 00:21:43.199
Commit offsets in onPartitionsRevoked.

345
00:21:44.549 --> 00:21:47.642
Retries, poison messages and dead-letter topics.

346
00:21:47.922 --> 00:21:53.042
A poison message fails every time: bad data, or a bug for one shape of input.

347
00:21:53.322 --> 00:21:57.887
With offset-based consumption, retrying it in place blocks the whole partition.

348
00:21:58.167 --> 00:21:59.042
L4:

349
00:21:59.322 --> 00:22:05.253
5 retries with 1 s backoff put 5,002 ms of lag on everything behind it.

350
00:22:06.603 --> 00:22:07.968
Here is a quick table:

351
00:22:08.248 --> 00:22:11.512
Strategy, and Behaviour, and L4 result, and Use when.

352
00:22:11.792 --> 00:22:13.477
Blocking retries (in place).

353
00:22:13.757 --> 00:22:14.653
Behaviour:

354
00:22:14.933 --> 00:22:17.131
Retry with backoff, partition waits.

355
00:22:17.411 --> 00:22:18.541
L4 result:

356
00:22:18.821 --> 00:22:21.339
Lag 5,002 ms, order kept.

357
00:22:21.619 --> 00:22:22.493
Use when:

358
00:22:22.773 --> 00:22:26.101
Transient errors (DB failover), strict ordering needed.

359
00:22:26.381 --> 00:22:28.173
Non-blocking retry topics.

360
00:22:28.453 --> 00:22:29.349
Behaviour:

361
00:22:29.629 --> 00:22:35.496
Publish to orders.placed.retry-1s,...retry-10s, then...dlt, and keep consuming.

362
00:22:35.776 --> 00:22:36.907
L4 result:

363
00:22:37.187 --> 00:22:40.003
Lag 2 ms, 1 event applied out of order.

364
00:22:40.283 --> 00:22:41.157
Use when:

365
00:22:41.437 --> 00:22:44.808
Independent events, or per-key order handled by versions.

366
00:22:45.088 --> 00:22:47.093
DLQ or DLT only.

367
00:22:47.373 --> 00:22:48.269
Behaviour:

368
00:22:48.549 --> 00:22:51.685
After N attempts, park it with error headers and alert.

369
00:22:51.965 --> 00:22:52.840
Use when:

370
00:22:53.120 --> 00:22:54.763
Always, as the last stop.

371
00:22:56.113 --> 00:22:56.966
Rules:

372
00:22:57.246 --> 00:23:07.273
Classify errors: retryable (timeouts, 5xx, deadlocks) versus non-retryable (validation, deserialization), then straight to the DLT.

373
00:23:07.553 --> 00:23:13.611
Cap attempts, and keep the original topic, partition, offset, exception and attempt count in headers.

374
00:23:13.891 --> 00:23:17.091
The DLT has an owner, an alert and a replay tool.

375
00:23:17.371 --> 00:23:21.190
A DLT nobody reads is data loss with extra steps.

376
00:23:22.540 --> 00:23:24.503
In queue systems this is native:

377
00:23:24.783 --> 00:23:39.396
JetStream's maxdeliver emitted an advisory after 3 deliveries (lab), RabbitMQ has dead-letter exchanges, SQS has redrive policies, and Redis needs a claim-count check (lab: moved to orders.dlq).

378
00:23:40.746 --> 00:23:42.239
Schemas and contracts.

379
00:23:42.519 --> 00:23:48.599
Events are a public API between teams, and they live longer than the code that wrote them (replay).

380
00:23:48.879 --> 00:23:50.095
Serialization:

381
00:23:50.375 --> 00:24:00.402
JSON (readable, no schema enforcement), Avro (compact, schema required, great evolution rules), Protobuf (compact, field numbers, gRPC-friendly).

382
00:24:00.682 --> 00:24:11.519
Schema registry (Confluent, Apicurio, Karapace): producers register schemas, records carry a schema id, and the registry enforces a compatibility mode:

383
00:24:12.869 --> 00:24:17.669
Backward: new consumers can read old data (add optional fields, remove fields).

384
00:24:17.949 --> 00:24:20.488
Forward: old consumers can read new data.

385
00:24:20.768 --> 00:24:21.792
Full: both.

386
00:24:22.072 --> 00:24:25.229
Evolution rules of thumb: add fields with defaults.

387
00:24:25.509 --> 00:24:27.621
Never reuse or renumber fields.

388
00:24:27.901 --> 00:24:29.480
Never change a field's meaning.

389
00:24:29.760 --> 00:24:36.117
Breaking changes go to a new versioned topic (order.order.placed.v2) with a dual-publish migration.

390
00:24:36.397 --> 00:24:37.421
Event design:

391
00:24:38.771 --> 00:24:42.910
A notification says something happened, go fetch, with a small payload.

392
00:24:43.190 --> 00:24:48.139
Event-carried state transfer includes the data consumers need, so they don't call back.

393
00:24:48.419 --> 00:24:49.934
Keep internal fields out.

394
00:24:50.214 --> 00:24:56.294
Include eventid, occurredat (HLC, Ch 12), aggregateid, version and schema version.

395
00:24:56.574 --> 00:25:00.307
Use CloudEvents-style envelopes where multiple teams publish.

396
00:25:00.587 --> 00:25:07.222
Ch 15 covers event types and event sourcing, and Ch 28 covers schema evolution in depth.

397
00:25:08.572 --> 00:25:09.895
Backpressure and lag.

398
00:25:10.175 --> 00:25:16.383
In a log, the broker never pushes more than a consumer asks for (pull model), so backpressure is natural.

399
00:25:16.663 --> 00:25:23.340
The symptom is consumer lag (latest offset minus committed offset), which is the first metric to alert on.

400
00:25:23.620 --> 00:25:30.383
Lag in time matters more than lag in records: alert on the oldest unprocessed event is 2 minutes old.

401
00:25:30.663 --> 00:25:33.180
Push-style systems need explicit limits:

402
00:25:33.460 --> 00:25:37.343
RabbitMQ prefetch (QoS), and JetStream maxackpending.

403
00:25:37.623 --> 00:25:42.721
The lab capped a slow consumer at 5 unacknowledged deliveries when it asked for 20.

404
00:25:43.001 --> 00:25:47.119
Everything else stays in the stream instead of piling up in consumer memory.

405
00:25:48.469 --> 00:26:00.821
Fixes for lag: more partitions and consumers (up to the partition count), faster processing (batching, async I or O), splitting a hot key, or shedding non-critical consumers during peaks.

406
00:26:01.101 --> 00:26:03.298
Retention is the deadline for lag.

407
00:26:03.578 --> 00:26:08.655
A group that falls behind by more than the retention window loses data silently.

408
00:26:08.935 --> 00:26:17.362
The Redis lab trimmed to 1,000 entries while groups lagged by ~3,000, so ~2,000 events would never reach them.

409
00:26:17.642 --> 00:26:20.330
Alert when lag in time approaches retention.

410
00:26:21.680 --> 00:26:23.216
Sizing and capacity.

411
00:26:23.496 --> 00:26:36.595
Partitions ≥ max(target throughput or per-partition producer throughput, target throughput or per-consumer throughput), plus headroom for 2 to 3 years (you can't shrink, and growing remaps keys).

412
00:26:36.875 --> 00:26:44.277
Typical numbers: tens of MB per second per partition for producers, and consumer throughput limited by your processing.

413
00:26:44.557 --> 00:26:52.749
Too many partitions costs memory, file handles, longer failovers and controller load (KRaft improves it, but it's still a cost).

414
00:26:53.029 --> 00:27:00.368
Storage = ingress bytes per second times retention times replication factor (+ ~20 % headroom).

415
00:27:00.648 --> 00:27:06.088
Tiered storage (KIP-405) moves old segments to object storage.

416
00:27:07.438 --> 00:27:22.158
For orders: ~1.3 K events per second times ~1 KB about 1.3 MB per second about 112 GB per day times 7 days times RF 3 about 2.4 TB.

417
00:27:22.438 --> 00:27:23.313
That's small.

418
00:27:23.593 --> 00:27:26.793
Throughput isn't the constraint, consumer parallelism is:

419
00:27:27.073 --> 00:27:31.318
24 partitions leaves room for 24 consumers per group.

420
00:27:32.668 --> 00:27:33.863
Choosing a broker.

421
00:27:34.143 --> 00:27:35.508
